-- title:
--   Kernel of reduction in G(𝒪) is p-divisible
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a commutative ring $R$ together with an algebra structure on a fixed algebraic closure $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}_p$ of $\mathbb{Q}_p$ (`PadicAlgCl p`), subject to the hypothesis `hO` that an element $x \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}_p$ is integral over $R$ exactly when $\|x\| \le 1$; write $\mathcal{O}$ for the integral closure of $R$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}_p$. Let $h$ be a natural number and $G$ a [`PDivisibleGroup R p h`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199), that is, a family of commutative $R$-Hopf algebras $G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ that are cocommutative, finite and free of rank $p^{vh}$ over $R$, together with surjective algebra-and-coalgebra maps $G.\mathrm{transition}\,v : G.\mathrm{level}(v+1) \to G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ whose kernels are the ideals generated by the image of the augmentation ideal under multiplication by $p^v$. Let $X$ be a completed point of $G$ over $\mathcal{O}$, i.e. a compatible family of elements $X_i$ of the direct limits $G.\mathrm{Points}(\mathcal{O}/p^i)$ over the levels, compatibility being with respect to the reduction maps $\mathcal{O}/p^{i+1} \to \mathcal{O}/p^i$. Assume `hX`: for every level $w$ and every $R$-algebra homomorphism $f : G.\mathrm{level}\,w \to \mathcal{O}/p$ (viewed in the convolution group of such maps) whose image in $G.\mathrm{Points}(\mathcal{O}/p)$ is the first component $X_1$ of $X$, and every $a \in G.\mathrm{level}\,w$, the element $f(a) - \varepsilon(a)$ is nilpotent, where $\varepsilon$ is the counit followed by the structure map $R \to \mathcal{O}/p$. Then there is a completed point $X'$ of $G$ over $\mathcal{O}$ satisfying the same nilpotence condition and with $p \cdot X' = X$.
--
--   The nilpotence condition on the reduction modulo $p$ singles out the subgroup $G^1$ of completed $\mathcal{O}$-valued points reducing to the identity in $G(\overline{\mathbb{F}}_p)$, i.e. the points of the connected component of $G$ with values in the maximal ideal of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{C}_p}$; the assertion is the $p$-divisibility of that subgroup, as in Tate's corollaries to his Proposition 4. It feeds into the Cartier-duality computation of $G(\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{C}_p})$ used in the study of $p$-divisible groups over $p$-adic integer rings.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_PDivisibleGroup_exists_nsmul_eq_of_forall_isNilpotent_cpointsProj_one_of_isIntegral_iff.lean

